.TH "COMPLYCTL\-PROVIDER\-OPENSCAP" "1" "September 2026" "ComplyTime Manual"
.SH NAME
complyctl\-provider\-openscap \- OpenSCAP scanning provider for
complyctl
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]complyctl\-provider\-openscap\f[R]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]complyctl\-provider\-openscap\f[R] is a gRPC plugin for
\f[B]complyctl\f[R](1) that provides compliance scanning capabilities
using the OpenSCAP engine.
It is not invoked directly by users; \f[B]complyctl\f[R] discovers and
launches it automatically when the provider is installed.
.PP
The provider receives assessment configurations from
\f[B]complyctl\f[R], generates XCCDF tailoring files matching the
requested profiles, executes scans via the \f[B]oscap\f[R] command\-line
tool, and returns structured results.
It supports automatic datastream selection via CPE\-based matching
against the host system.
.PP
Communication with \f[B]complyctl\f[R] uses the hashicorp/go\-plugin
gRPC interface, implementing three RPCs: \f[B]Describe\f[R],
\f[B]Generate\f[R], and \f[B]Scan\f[R].
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration is provided through variables defined in assessment plan
targets.
These variables are passed to the provider via \f[B]complyctl\f[R] and
are not set manually by the user.
.TP
\f[B]profile\f[R] (required)
XCCDF profile ID to evaluate.
Must match the pattern \f[B][a\-zA\-Z0\-9\-_.]+\f[R].
Examples: \f[B]cis_server_l1\f[R],
\f[B]xccdf_org.ssgproject.content_profile_cis\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]datastream\f[R] (optional)
Path to an SSG XCCDF datastream XML file.
Relative paths resolve against the selected workspace; absolute external
paths remain absolute.
When omitted, the provider auto\-detects the appropriate datastream by
matching the system\(cqs CPE (from the os\-release file) against CPE
metadata embedded in the SSG datastream files.
.TP
\f[B]workspace\f[R] (host supplied)
Coordinate and artifact root supplied through Generate global variables
and Scan target variables.
\f[B]complyctl\f[R] selects \f[B]\(enworkspace\f[R], then
\f[B]COMPLYTIME_WORKSPACE\f[R], then the invoking directory.
Providers use the supplied value without repeating that precedence.
Missing/empty values fall back to provider CWD for direct callers;
invalid or conflicting roots fail the RPC.
Relative workspace values resolve against provider CWD, including root
symlinks.
Tool subprocesses use the resolved workspace without changing provider
CWD.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\f[B]SSG_CONTENT_DIR\f[R]
Directory containing SSG datastream XML files.
Used during auto\-detection when \f[B]datastream\f[R] is not specified.
Default: \f[B]/usr/share/xml/scap/ssg/content\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]OS_RELEASE_FILE\f[R]
Path to the os\-release file used for CPE extraction during datastream
auto\-detection.
Default: \f[B]/etc/os\-release\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]COMPLYTIME_MACHINE_ID_FILE\f[R]
Path to the machine\-id file used for host identification in evidence
remarks.
The file content is included in each Evidence.Source.Remarks field
alongside the hostname so auditors can identify the host where the scan
was executed.
Default: \f[B]/etc/machine\-id\f[R]
.SH EXTERNAL TOOLS
Requires \f[B]oscap\f[R] (provided by the \f[B]openscap\-scanner\f[R]
package) at runtime.
The provider locates \f[B]oscap\f[R] via \f[B]PATH\f[R] lookup.
.PP
The \f[B]scap\-security\-guide\f[R] package provides the SSG datastream
files used for evaluation.
.SH FILES
.TP
\f[I].complytime/openscap/\f[R]
Provider workspace directory.
All paths below are relative to the selected workspace root, not the log
directory or the reader\(cqs CWD.
.TP
\f[I].complytime/openscap/policy/tailoring.xml\f[R]
XCCDF tailoring file generated by the \f[B]Generate\f[R] RPC.
Contains the selected profile rules matched from the assessment
configuration.
.TP
\f[I].complytime/openscap/results/<target>/<profile>/evaluation\-<time>\-<token>/arf.xml\f[R]
Asset Reporting Format (ARF) result file produced by \f[B]oscap xccdf
eval \(enresults\-arf\f[R] during the \f[B]Scan\f[R] RPC.
.TP
\f[I].complytime/openscap/results/<target>/<profile>/evaluation\-<time>\-<token>/results.xml\f[R]
XCCDF results XML file produced alongside the ARF file by \f[B]oscap
xccdf eval \(enresults\f[R].
.TP
\f[I].complytime/openscap/results/<target>/<profile>/evaluation\-<time>\-<token>/metadata.json\f[R]
Best\-effort supporting context: request target, observed ARF target,
profile, datastream and tailoring paths.
This file is not evidence authority and contains no artifact digests.
Internal provenance paths are workspace\-relative; external paths remain
absolute.
Metadata write/close failures warn without suppressing otherwise
complete evidence; the file can be absent or incomplete.
.PP
Target/profile labels are bounded readable grouping hints, not unique
identities.
An absent request target uses \f[B]system\f[R].
Time uses UTC \f[B]YYYYMMDDTHHMMSSZ\f[R]; each token has at least 128
bits of cryptographic entropy.
Generate\-time tailoring and remediation paths above remain replaceable,
not historical policy snapshots.
.TP
\f[I].complytime/openscap/remediations/remediation\-script.sh\f[R]
Bash remediation script generated by \f[B]oscap xccdf generate fix\f[R]
during the \f[B]Generate\f[R] RPC.
.TP
\f[I].complytime/openscap/remediations/remediation\-playbook.yml\f[R]
Ansible remediation playbook generated by \f[B]oscap xccdf generate
fix\f[R].
.TP
\f[I].complytime/openscap/remediations/remediation\-blueprint.toml\f[R]
Blueprint remediation file generated by \f[B]oscap xccdf generate
fix\f[R].
.SH EVIDENCE
The provider records an \f[B]Evidence\f[R] entry on each
\f[B]AssessmentLog\f[R] returned by the \f[B]Scan\f[R] RPC:
.TP
\f[B]Type\f[R]
\f[B]ARF\f[R] \(em Asset Reporting Format.
The ARF file aggregates XCCDF results, OVAL definitions, and system
characteristics into a single SCAP\-standard artifact.
.TP
\f[B]Production\f[R]
The ARF file is produced by running \f[B]oscap xccdf eval
\(enresults\-arf\f[R] against the tailoring file and the SSG datastream.
It is written to the fresh target/profile evaluation directory shown
under \f[B]FILES\f[R], alongside required supporting XCCDF output.
Valid noncompliant results are retained too.
.TP
\f[B]EvaluationLog representation\f[R]
Each assessment log carries an Evidence entry with:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]ID\f[R]: \f[B]arf_<token>\f[R], shared by assessments from one
evaluation
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]CollectedAt\f[R]: UTC RFC 3339 collection start, captured before
tool execution
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Source.Coordinate\f[R]: selected\-workspace\-relative path to
retained \f[B]arf.xml\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Source.Digest\f[R]: \f[B]sha256:<hex>\f[R] digest of the ARF file
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Source.ReferenceID\f[R]: \f[B]openscap\-arf_<token>\f[R], matching
a mapping reference with an escaped absolute \f[B]file://\f[R] URL to
the same artifact
.RE
.PP
The Gemara Evaluation Log alone defines the evidence set through its
evidence entries and mapping references.
Supporting XCCDF and metadata files are not implicitly evidence.
Resolve each coordinate against the selected workspace root, or use its
absolute file URL, and check the file\(cqs SHA256 digest against the
log.
URI escaping preserves spaces, percent signs and non\-ASCII names.
Absolute URLs describe the originating filesystem; copying the workspace
preserves relative coordinates but does not relocate those URLs.
.PP
Preservation is always on, with no automatic cleanup or retention
settings.
Each scan stores a separate output set even when bytes match.
Storage grows with scans, including unreferenced partial outputs left by
failures.
Allocation, required output/close, parsing, hashing and finalization
errors fail the RPC without a response; no earlier fixed\-path result
substitutes for missing output.
.PP
Evaluation directories are \f[B]0700\f[R] and evidence/supporting files
are \f[B]0600\f[R], owned by the provider execution account.
Sudo and direct\-root scans keep root\-owned evidence; the original
non\-root user needs authorized elevated reading/hashing.
Environment or RPC ownership claims do not select another owner.
The account needs a writable workspace and traversal access through all
parents; new context parents permit traversal without widening
evaluation leaves or changing unrelated directories.
For permission errors, verify the execution account and parent access.
For disk\-space errors, provision sufficient writable capacity.
Do not widen private evidence modes or assume partial outputs are
published evidence.
.PP
Preserve the Evaluation Log and its referenced files separately:
uploading only the log does not transfer artifact bytes.
Downstream collection must preserve both, keep log\-selected files
retrievable, verify transferred bytes against logged digests, and map
moved artifacts to original references without modifying the log.
Collection, validation of collected copies and optional packaging are
separate future complyctl capabilities, not implemented here.
Storage backends and longevity remain customer\-owned.
.PP
\f[B]Breaking upgrade behavior:\f[R] paths and evidence/reference IDs
are now evaluation\-qualified, without fixed\-path aliases.
Relative input/configuration lookup now uses the selected workspace
rather than invocation CWD; migrate invocation\-relative configurations.
Existing fixed\-path outputs stop being refreshed; they and historical
logs remain untouched and are not migrated.
Already overwritten evidence cannot be recovered.
Older binaries resume overwrite behavior on rollback while retained
evaluation directories remain readable by authorized users.
.SH EXIT CODES
This provider is a gRPC subprocess managed by \f[B]complyctl\f[R].
Exit codes follow the hashicorp/go\-plugin protocol and are not
user\-facing.
